<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zawila</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zawila is an oasis town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fezzan">Fezzan</a> region of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murzuq">Murzuq</a>. In the early Islamic period it was the chief town of the Fezzan and a major station on the caravan route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, through which enslaved people were brought north. From about 918 it was the seat of the Ibadi Banu Khattab dynasty, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharaf-al-din-qaraqush">Sharaf al-Din Qaraqush</a> conquered it in the 1170s.</p></div>
